WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.010
MEHRAN NAZIR: Welcome to Stream
Work Makes the Dream Work.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.000
Streaming on Linux is hard,
but it doesn't have to be.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.290
Data flow simplifies it for you.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:23.750
You can start today, and you
don't have to be a developer.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.820
We're going to cover
three topics today--

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:31.550
how do I start, how do I
scale, and how do I optimize.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.600
But firstly, who are you?

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.880
Someone with data
in your title--

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.720
data engineer, data
analyst, data scientist,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.320
someone who knows that real time
data is the key to unlocking

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.578
new experiences?

00:00:46.578 --> 00:00:48.370
Someone who wants to
start using the newest

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.970
features and isn't afraid
to use some of the products

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.670
that we are going to
be showcasing today?

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.820
And someone who might
be familiar with SQL?

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.810
Python and Java are a
bonus but not required.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.407
My name is Mehran Nazir,
and I'm a product manager

00:01:05.407 --> 00:01:05.990
with Dataflow.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.860
Before we dive into
the presentation,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.930
let's introduce Beam and
Dataflow for our newcomers.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.040
Beaming and Dataflow
work together.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.460
Beam is the open source SDK
used to express [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.550
processing pipeline, while
Dataflow is the execution

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.220
engine.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.800
Beam has an open source
programming model by the Apache

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.120
project, is a top Apache
project by developer activity,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.140
includes a unified
backing streaming model.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.960
So for folks that are coming
from the [INAUDIBLE] world

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.200
and have to maintain
two different systems,

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.000
you just have one--

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.570
and offers a unique flexibility
with Runner and languages,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.330
meaning you could
write your pipelines

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.780
in the SDK of your choice,
with the desired backend

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.060
of your choice.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.240
Dataflow, on the other hand,
is an execution engine that

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.740
runs Apache Beam pipelines.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.190
It's fully managed
in serverless and can

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:07.290
scale to millions of QPS with
no management and no tuning.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.590
It offers accuracy, with
exactly-once streaming

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:14.490
guarantees and
offers the separation

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.585
of compute and storage with our
shuffle and streaming engine

00:02:17.585 --> 00:02:18.085
services.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.910
So let's get into this.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.010
How do I start?

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.370
To motivate this
journey, we're going

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:33.420
to take the perspective of a
data scientist named Serena.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.390
Serena is a data scientist
that analyzes data

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.400
produced by data pipelines.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.030
She creates and maintains
reports and dashboards--

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.000
and sets up and maintains the
company's data architecture.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.600
Some of the tools and
skills she has include

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.380
SQL, Python, in particular
libraries like scikit

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.260
learn, pandas, matplotlib.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.320
And she likes using Jupyter
Notebooks to prototype

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.760
and explore data ad hoc.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.220
But Serena's tasks
come with challenges.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.770
She has to run these analyses
on a scheduled basis,

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.020
whether that be daily,
weekly, monthly.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.260
And that creates a fair
bit of manual toil.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.350
She has to interface with a
number of different production

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.840
systems, some of which
she's not familiar with.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.210
And sharing pipeline code
is manual and subject

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.220
to human error.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.855
And then, if data
doesn't arrive on time,

00:03:29.855 --> 00:03:32.230
she doesn't have the skills
to optimize and troubleshoot,

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.590
if necessary.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.890
Serena discovers steaming
analytics with Dataflow,

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:42.150
but in taking a
look at it, she's

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.600
immediately inundated with
a lot of new information.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.960
She has to learn about a
new Beam programming model.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.030
She has to understand
what Runners are--

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.670
and hearing terms like
Dataflow, Flink, Spark.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.080
She's new to concepts
like windowing,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.160
watermarks, and triggers.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.530
And ideally, she'd have
a sandbox environment

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.730
to test these concepts.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.140
Enter Jupyter Notebooks, which
is perfect for her use case.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.540
She's familiar with them,
and it allows her a place

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.230
to learn about Beam concepts as
she iteratively builds her Beam

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.230
pipeline in a safe environment.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.779
Usually, notebooks have been
good for analyzing batch data

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:24.170
sets, but Dataflow has built
new streaming extensions

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:26.490
for notebook environments.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.520
Interactive Beam will bring
new functionality that

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:35.340
allow you to record sources, as
well as interactively explore

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.560
intermediate data
transformations,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.930
called PCollections.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:43.020
This will help new learners
pick up the basics of Beam.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.680
But not only that, it'll
help experienced developers

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.690
prototype pipelines step by
step in a read eval print loop

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.323
workflow.

00:04:54.323 --> 00:04:55.490
So let's see this in action.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.580
To get started with
Beam notebooks,

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.000
Serena will navigate to
the Dataflow console--

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.673
and using the left hand panel,
navigate to the notebooks page.

00:05:09.673 --> 00:05:11.090
She'll see a list
of the notebooks

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.300
she's created, specifically
with the Apache environment.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.460
And if this screen
looks familiar to her,

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.920
she'll realize that this is
actually identical to the AI

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.110
platform notebooks interface.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:24.000
Beam notebooks is built
on top of that platform,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.170
meaning any features available
in AI Platform notebooks

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:31.565
is also available
through Beam notebooks.

00:05:31.565 --> 00:05:33.190
She'll click on the
New Instance button

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:40.620
at the top, which will show an
option for an Apache Beam VM.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.420
What this VM will
include will be

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.170
all the necessary Beam
libraries and packages

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.802
for the interactive
capabilities,

00:05:47.802 --> 00:05:49.260
as well as some
examples that we'll

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.400
go through in just a moment.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.740
You have the ability to
select your region, your zone,

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:57.240
and network, if desired.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.080
And I'm ready, I can hit Create.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.240
That process takes a few
minutes to initialize,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.680
so what we'll do
instead is to navigate

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.950
to a JupyterLab instance
that has already

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.480
been created for Serena.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.630
So I'm going to click
on the open JupyterLab

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:20.900
tab, which will navigate
to the Apache Beam notebook

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.800
experience.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.680
And what you'll see when
you first start is a read

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.840
me that includes a
directory of examples.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.460
We pre-populated
the Beam containers

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:35.400
with four examples
that successively

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.850
build on one another.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.450
The first one is a word
count that demonstrates

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.570
a simple batch pipeline.

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:44.490
It's the second example
is an extension of that

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.470
into streaming, where
as opposed to reading

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.290
from a batch data
source, it is reading

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:54.660
from a streaming Pub/Sub topic.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.520
And the third example is more
advanced streaming use case,

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:03.490
where we are looking at taxi
ride data coming New York City.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:05.200
And then lastly,
we have a notebook

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.740
that shows how you launch a
Dataflow job from the notebook

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:10.530
environment.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:15.320
So as I enter the
directory of examples

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.170
and really focus my attention
on streaming word count as well

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.020
as the Dataflow
word count, I want

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.280
to level us at the five
things that Serena is really

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.208
looking for in her first
foray into streaming.

00:07:26.208 --> 00:07:27.500
She wants to learn five things.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:31.380
She wants to learn one, how to
read from a streaming source.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.160
Two, she wants to better
understand windowing

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:37.460
and how to explore that
data in the context of Beam.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:43.600
Three, she want to learn how to
write that to a streaming sync.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.695
Forth, she wants to see what
that graph will look like--

00:07:46.695 --> 00:07:49.070
that topology, what will it
look like when it's submitted

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.520
to the Dataflow runner.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.820
And then lastly, she
wants to know, again,

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.490
how can she launch what's
created in the notebook

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:59.320
environment into the
Dataflow service.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.930
The streaming word count
example is a good starting point

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:05.022
for understanding
how she can do that.

00:08:05.022 --> 00:08:06.730
So the first thing
she'll do is obviously

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:10.660
she will import these
libraries, all the relevant ones

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:15.790
for Apache Beam, as well as
some other utility libraries.

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:18.850
And after that, she's ready
to start setting up options

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:23.180
to create the
streaming pipeline.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:26.510
The next cell she'll
see is something

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:29.840
she's not familiar with, an
interactive option called

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:31.190
capture duration.

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:35.000
What that allows you to do is
set the data capture duration

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.830
for your source recording.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:38.419
It can be set in
terms of seconds.

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.220
It could also be set
in terms of size.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:45.980
But again, it gives you the
ability to assign how much data

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.620
you want to be able to capture
from your streaming source--

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:53.070
again, given that streaming
sources are infinite,

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.530
this allows you to
allow a cap for that.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.200
Another important
option that we've also

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:04.430
introduced for streaming
data is around the ability

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:07.950
to enable capture replay.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:11.370
What this will allow you
to do is, if you always

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.880
want to see these freshest data,
you could set this to false--

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.920
so that all the data is new.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.477
But as you might know, one
of the difficult things

00:09:19.477 --> 00:09:21.060
about notebook
environments in general

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.390
is, how do I recreate streaming
experiments, especially when

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:28.530
data is coming in all the time.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.030
The ability to
reproduce experiments

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.840
is already difficult. The
ability to turn this to true

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.080
allows you to, again, turn
a streaming source basically

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:38.340
into a batch--

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:41.110
and allows you to project
on one data source to,

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:46.290
again, set a constant,
in terms of the data that

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:47.833
is being ingested.

00:09:47.833 --> 00:09:50.250
So now that we've talked a
little bit about what you could

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.070
do with source recording,
let's go actually

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:55.860
into the meat of this
pipeline-- and actually start

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.815
building it from the ground up.

00:09:57.815 --> 00:09:59.190
The first thing
you'll see is I'm

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:02.700
going to be able to read
words from this Pub/Sub

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.481
transformation.

00:10:04.481 --> 00:10:06.700
I've aligned this
cell, and you'll

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:08.440
see that this read
is essentially

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.380
going to map to the transformed,
when I actually submit it

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:12.918
to the Dataflow runner.

00:10:12.918 --> 00:10:14.710
The first thing I do
when I see those words

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:16.510
is, I want to be able
to windows those.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:19.940
I want to be able to assign
each to specific time windows.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.610
And as you can tell
from this cell,

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:25.690
I am assigning them
to 10 second windows.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:27.890
And what I want to do
with each of those windows

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:31.670
is count the number
of occurrences of each

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.470
of these words per window.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.220
And that's what the
next cell will do.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:39.250
So now that I've basically
created the first three

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:41.178
transformations
in my pipeline, I

00:10:41.178 --> 00:10:42.970
want to take a chance
to actually now start

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:46.520
understanding what I'm
doing and explore my data.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.660
So that's what I have
the IB.show function.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:51.160
This, again, is a
functionality that

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.030
allows you to include window
information as a parameter.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.480
And not only will you
see the number of times

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:01.600
particular words are being
counted in a given window,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.540
but you'll also see the
specific window panes

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.110
that they are associated with.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:08.670
As I scroll down,
you'll also see

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.440
that I have the ability
to also visualize

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.730
data that is coming in through
the streaming data source.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:20.680
And we've built a few helpful
tools that will allow you,

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:24.640
again, visualize the
distribution, as well

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:30.280
as the occurrence of these words
in a more illustrative context.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.560
What I want to do now
is take that word--

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:39.050
again, I want to be
able to count based

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.000
on a normalized set of counts--

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.550
and not, again, necessarily
count based on capitalization.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:48.800
So what I'll create is a map
function that lower cases each

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:49.850
of the words.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.940
And once I've done that, I
use the show function again

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:57.340
to count the number of
words that I'm seeing

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:59.348
in each of these windows.

00:11:59.348 --> 00:12:00.890
And the last thing
that we've created

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:03.720
is a new function
called collect,

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:06.200
which allows you
to cast that data

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:09.310
into the form of an
appendaged data frame

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:12.350
and allow you to interact with
that data in a context that's

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:13.550
a lot more familiar.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.050
So now that I've, kind
of, taken a look at--

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.627
one, how to read from
a streaming source,

00:12:20.627 --> 00:12:22.460
now that I understand
that what windowing is

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:23.668
and how to explore that data.

00:12:23.668 --> 00:12:26.482
And I've now gotten a
chance to do those things,

00:12:26.482 --> 00:12:28.940
now I want to start actually
thinking about how I launch it

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:30.065
in the [INAUDIBLE] service.

00:12:30.065 --> 00:12:32.180
And what I'll do here
is, I'll switch over

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:33.680
to the data for
word count example.

00:12:36.793 --> 00:12:38.210
When I click on
this-- again, this

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:41.510
is this repeating the
word count examples

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:43.610
from the initial examples.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:48.110
But all quickly jump into
the interesting parts.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:50.870
Now that I am thinking about
launching into the Dataflow

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.480
service, I want to think about
actually writing this data

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:55.670
to an output data set.

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.610
And by going through
these specific cells,

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:03.140
you'll see that I will write
this to GCS after I specified

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:05.690
a few options.

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:09.260
You'll see that I've now
indicated GS bucket, which

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:16.460
will contain the output of
this streaming data pipeline.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:21.440
And once I have created this out
of this write transformation,

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.780
which will write
this to text file

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:27.110
and write it to a
specific GCS location,

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:29.750
I want to now see what will
this look like on the Dataflow

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:31.400
service.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.420
That's where I'll use the
IB.show graph function, which

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:38.950
will give me an example of what
my topology will look like.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:41.600
And then, as you can tell, it's
exactly as I've described it.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:44.090
You'll read data from
a Pub/Sub source,

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:47.930
that will cast it to a specific
peak collection called words.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.480
I'll count those and write
them to cloud storage,

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.240
but I'll also create a
separate branch which

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:57.440
will lowercase the words and
count unique instances that are

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:01.280
irrespective of capitalization.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.710
Now that I've, again,
verified that graph,

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:08.450
I can now use the Dataflow
runner to run that pipeline.

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:12.460
And when I run this
cell right here,

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:18.740
I can now quickly click here
and navigate specifically

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:24.150
to the job that I've written
in the Dataflow console.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:32.740
And with that, Serena now has
gotten the entire top to bottom

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:38.320
with streaming data sources,
using a Beam notebook.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:41.200
Serena doesn't only
have to use notebooks.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:44.140
She can also use SQL to
launch streaming jobs.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:47.230
Dataflow SQL, a GA
capability, allows

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.820
you to launch Dataflow pipelines
from the BigQuery you want.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:54.530
She can select sources and
sinks from Pub/Sub, to BigQuery,

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:55.460
to GSA.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:59.510
It has a visual schema editor
to help inspect the data.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:02.450
Some of the cool things she can
do with this capability include

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.970
real time dashboards
from BigQuery,

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:10.070
as well as create other sources
for other streaming pipelines.

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:13.160
Now that Serena has
mastered the fundamentals,

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:16.180
how does she scale?

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:17.950
Her team wants to
use streaming now

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:21.280
for data transport, simple
transformation needs,

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:23.010
as well as real time analytics.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.540
But her teammates have
different requirements.

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:29.020
They all have different sources
and sinks, not all of them

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:30.790
have programming
skills, and they all

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:32.770
prefer to work from
different environments,

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:37.840
whether that be the UI, the
command line, or the 90E.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.660
The solution?

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:42.350
Serena can use Flex templates,
a feature that we have recently

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:44.570
launched into beta.

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:47.000
With Flex templates, she
can turn any pipeline

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:51.080
into a template that can
be used by other teammates.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:53.540
You just can take that
template and execute it

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.700
with their own arbitrary
parameters passed in.

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:59.690
They could do this from the
UI, from the command line,

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:02.855
or by passing in a
request with the REST API.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:08.100
We'll now go through a walk
through of Flex templates,

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.500
which will show how we
containerize the Dataflow

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.780
pipeline, building the
Flex template itself,

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:15.030
then launching it from the UI.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:21.380
Before Serena can use Flex
templates to share her pipeline

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:24.403
with her teammates, she has
to first author a pipeline.

00:16:24.403 --> 00:16:25.820
So we'll start our
demo by looking

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:28.170
at the pipeline she's authored.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.800
This is a Python
template available online

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:36.890
that is ingesting a stream of
positive and negative reviews.

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:40.020
And as you can look at the
statistics helper function,

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:44.980
it is returning URL, the number
of reviews returned by a URL,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:49.120
an average score for those
URLs, and the oldest and most

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:52.760
recent version of those reviews.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:54.610
And if you look at
the pipeline itself,

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:58.770
you'll see it's a very
simple read from Pub/Sub,

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:02.140
a transcription of
bytes to string,

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.190
as well as a parsing of
the JSON messages itself.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:08.579
It windows that data
into given intervals

00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:12.660
that will slice that data
in the specified window

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.859
duration, group those URLs, and
then run that statistic helper

00:17:16.859 --> 00:17:18.310
function.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:21.530
And as you can tell,
there are a list

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:24.410
of arguments that are listed
at the end of the pipeline that

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:29.262
are going to be used for passing
in as a template parameter.

00:17:29.262 --> 00:17:30.970
So now that we've
looked at the pipeline,

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:34.880
we're going to use Docker
to build that template.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:37.590
So I'm going to switch over
to the shell window and copy

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:41.600
and paste a build command.

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:44.480
And what this is doing
is building that pipeline

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.940
into a container.

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:49.880
This G Cloud command is
essentially roughly equivalent

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:54.880
to authenticating into
the Docker service,

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:58.880
building that Docker
container using the image,

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:02.570
as well as pushing that to
the Google Container Registry.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:04.520
And once we are
concluded, you'll

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.600
see that our pipeline
has successfully been

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:12.010
turned into a Docker container.

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:14.520
So now that we have it in
the container registry,

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:18.390
we are one step short of
turning it into a Flux template.

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:22.290
Before we can do that, we have
to create a template spec file,

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:24.640
which includes the
name, description,

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:26.250
and input parameters.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:29.070
So I'll flip back
to my text editor

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:33.340
and see what this container
spec file looks like.

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:36.330
You'll see that it has
a name, a description,

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:42.360
as well as a name label and help
text for the given parameters.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:44.550
What my parameters
will include will

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.660
be a Pub/Sub input subscription,
a BigQuery output table,

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:51.450
as well as a user
specified window interval.

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:55.680
As you can also tell, I can also
create some validation around

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:59.880
whether these specific
parameters are optional or not.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:02.750
So now that we're ready
to actually build the flux

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:10.860
template, I'm to quickly copy
and paste my Flex template

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:14.720
command, and I will
go back to my shell.

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.630
And once again, I will use a
G Cloud beta command, which

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:19.760
was recently created
for Flex templates,

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.800
and build a container.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.670
And what we'll see,
in just a few moments,

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.700
is successfully saving
this container spec

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:27.750
in this Flux template
file, which I will

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:31.130
be allowed to call in the UI.

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:37.740
So I can copy and paste
this directory right here

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:40.900
and go back to the UI.

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:42.940
So I'm back in the
Dataflow console,

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:46.440
and in the jobs list page,
I can see at the very top

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.220
a button for creating
a job from template.

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.070
I'm going to navigate to that.

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.750
And I'll see that I have the
ability to specify a name.

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:58.270
So we'll call this Serena
teammate Pub/Sub to BigQuery.

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:06.590
And I have the ability
to select a template.

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.690
What you also see is a
list of Google provided

00:20:08.690 --> 00:20:10.850
templates, which allow
you to do some very

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:14.780
simple things for streaming,
as well as batch and utility

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:15.650
functions.

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:17.490
But for the sake
of this example,

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:20.600
we're going to focus primarily
on the custom template.

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.970
If you remember, we
have a GS location

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:28.380
for that Flex template.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:30.528
I'm going to copy and
paste that into here.

00:20:30.528 --> 00:20:31.570
And once I've seen that--

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:33.120
I've seen that
validate, I'll see

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:36.210
that description that I specify
in the container spec file

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:39.270
is located here, and the
required parameters are

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.560
found right here.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.860
And now, my teammates can
now use the same template

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:47.580
that I've created, on behalf
of, Serena with the organization

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:54.210
to, again, drive, standardize,
and more broadly, enable

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:56.670
Dataflow for a
streaming organization.

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:03.040
And in the event that you do not
want to confine this to the UI,

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:04.830
you'll see at the
very bottom, we

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:09.530
have also the ability to
create the equivalent rest,

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:12.050
as well as the
equivalent command line.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:14.270
So again, if you want to be
able to launch a Dataflow

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:16.520
job from your own
environment, you

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:19.550
have the ability to also just
do that directly from the UI

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:23.021
and enable your organization
to use Flex templates.

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:29.260
Serena has now solidified
her Dataflux expertise,

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:31.870
and she's been able to
share it with teammates.

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:33.580
The final mission for her--

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:37.890
how does she optimize
her pipelines?

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:40.140
Serena needs various
things to help

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.330
understand what is
happening in her data

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:45.120
processing environment,
monitoring observability

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:49.290
tools, logging,
statistics and counters,

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:52.230
and alerts to know if
pipelines fall behind

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:55.250
or violate their SLOs.

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:57.290
The great thing
about Dataflow is,

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:59.990
we offer these tools
right out of the box.

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:02.480
We all very rich
observability experience

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:05.340
that includes relative
time series metrics,

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:08.220
logs, and configuration
information.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:11.420
And we've also recently
launched a manage pipelines view

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:14.330
to help you understand how your
recurring Dataflow jobs are

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.300
running and quickly identify
if your data sets are not

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.360
being processed in time.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:24.130
So let's flip back to the user
interface for our third demo.

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:30.530
To demonstrate how we're going
to use the new observability

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:33.870
experience to optimize
your Dataflow job,

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:37.020
we're going to navigate
to the Dataflow console.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:39.310
The first screen you'll
see is the jobs list page,

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:43.330
which, again, is a listing of
all your jobs, sorted by type

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:47.170
as well as status and the
ability to filter those jobs.

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:48.940
We're going to
focus our attention

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.600
on a retail application
that we've open sourced.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.160
What this application
is doing is reading data

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.600
from a website in mobile apps.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.720
It is also collecting
transaction information

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.780
from on prem systems, like a
brick and mortar location, as

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:04.060
well as collecting
information from inventory

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:07.030
to track the inventory
levels of a specific product.

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:11.491
And its joining that information
in running computations.

00:23:11.491 --> 00:23:14.250
I'm going to click on that job.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:16.850
And it's loading, I'll also
mention that this application

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:18.270
is fully open source.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.240
So I do encourage you
to look at the source

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:24.080
code for this
application for yourself.

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.510
The first thing you'll
see is the job graph.

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:29.630
The job graph is the data
folder's interpretation

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:31.100
of your business logic.

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:33.170
It presents this
topology for you,

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:37.640
to, again, review the
entirety of your application.

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:40.280
As I've already mentioned,
what this application is doing

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:42.210
is reading from
multiple sources.

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:44.390
It's processing
multiple streams of data

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:47.090
and writing to different syncs.

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:49.820
At the very top, you'll
see the re-transformations.

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:52.370
You'll see that it's reading
a click stream of data coming

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:56.720
from websites and mobile apps--

00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:58.970
a transaction stream,
which is reading data

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:00.800
from on prem systems--

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:02.600
for example, brick
and mortar locations.

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:04.820
And then lastly,
a stream of data

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:09.050
that is tracking the levels
of inventory available.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:12.280
Each of these streams have
respective processing buckets.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:15.320
And at the very end,
you'll see, after a number

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:18.320
of different computations are
made, as well as some counts,

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:22.040
we are writing to a
sync, like BigQuery,

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:26.270
for the consumption of
another team to run analysis,

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:30.980
as well as to Pub/Sub for
consumption by other pipelines.

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:34.830
You'll see a little arrow
next to some of these boxes.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.610
And what that's really
doing is condensing

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:39.290
a lot of information.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.450
When I click on the
Process Click Stream,

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:44.210
you'll see a pretty
significant expansion,

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:47.720
with an explanation of what
each of these steps are doing.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:50.810
Again, this is business logic
that's been expressed by you,

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:54.650
and you'll see things like
clean data, mapping elements,

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:57.860
filtering that data, as
well as computing account--

00:24:57.860 --> 00:25:01.070
to see which products
are selling the best.

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:05.012
And then once that combination
is made, writing to BigQuery.

00:25:05.012 --> 00:25:06.470
But if I want to
get a better sense

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:10.230
for how specific
steps are performing,

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.210
I can click on that step.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:13.850
And what you'll see
on the right here

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:17.270
is step information,
information with regard

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:20.330
to the throughput of
that data, as well as

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:21.830
the output collections.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.740
Again, you'll be able
to identify specifically

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:27.950
what steps might be problematic
and dig a little deeper

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:30.462
from there.

00:25:30.462 --> 00:25:32.170
And one last thing
I'll say about the job

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:33.820
graph in this
specific splash page

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:36.645
is, you'll have a Job Info
panel on the right, which

00:25:36.645 --> 00:25:38.710
will allow you to collect
important metadata

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:45.670
about your job, including job
ID, the SDK version, location--

00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:47.800
specific information with
regards to the metrics,

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:50.380
the resources being consumed.

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:52.900
This will have a direct
relation to the cost

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:56.800
of the pipeline itself, as
well as the pipeline options

00:25:56.800 --> 00:26:00.940
that you've selected
for your pipeline.

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:04.235
We've recently announced
a job metrics tab.

00:26:04.235 --> 00:26:05.610
And this is what
we are referring

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:07.588
to as the observability dash.

00:26:07.588 --> 00:26:09.630
This is what you want to
use to optimize your job

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:13.230
but also to diagnose
issues that might arise.

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:16.650
You'll see specific
graphs that are in line--

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:19.540
data freshness showing
the watermark of your data

00:26:19.540 --> 00:26:21.960
and to identify whether
data is falling behind

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:23.640
for whatever reason.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:25.380
System latency, which
will show, again,

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:28.470
if there's a specific
bottleneck in your system.

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:29.870
You'll see an auto
scaling graph.

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:32.230
This specific application
is only showing one worker.

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:35.340
So it's not really showing
the dynamism of Dataflow,

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:37.830
but if you have an
application that

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:41.712
is looking at multiple workers
with varying degrees of volume,

00:26:41.712 --> 00:26:43.920
that will also show in the
amount of workers that are

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.590
being provisioned for your job.

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:48.090
You'll also see
the same throughput

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.960
that we mentioned
on a per step level.

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:53.370
But what's very helpful
about this specific table

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:56.910
is that now you are looking
at the entirety of your job--

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:02.560
and now the ability to sort
your steps by throughput,

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:04.170
and then quickly
identify specifically

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:08.670
where you might have bottlenecks
in your business logic.

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:10.500
You'll see CPU
utilization, which

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:15.590
will also chart the amount
of CPU being used by workers.

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:17.700
And then lastly, you'll
see a worker air lock

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:22.050
count, which, again, allows you
to see where specific errors

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:24.570
are occurring on your workers.

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:27.600
I think another
good tip here is,

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:30.570
you'll see that, when
I hover over the chart,

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:35.160
I'll see a gray dashed line,
which will also showcase itself

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:38.830
in the charts above,
in CPU realization.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:41.010
And what that allows you
to do is very quickly

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:44.250
draw a correlation between when
an error might be happening

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.860
and what might be happening
on your CPU utilization,

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:50.280
as well as your throughput.

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:52.080
Another tool that I'll
also mention in this

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:54.020
is the time selecting tool.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:55.860
And by default,
you'll see six hours.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:28:00.210
But you can actually look
back as far as 730 days.

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:06.680
We'll just take a look at your
data over the last seven days.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:11.850
And what you'll see is a
varying degree of throughput,

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:14.220
just as you would see
as a retail application,

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:17.950
you'll see varying degrees
of volume of user traffic.

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:22.050
And that's represented in this
graph, in the throughput graph.

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:25.470
Again, if you more prefer to
see the amount of data that's

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:28.600
being written in terms
of gigabytes in size,

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:30.300
you also have the
ability to select

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:33.190
estimated bytes per second.

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:34.780
And the last thing
I'll also mention

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:40.720
is, we also have a logs
panel that includes job logs,

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:44.350
worker logs, and a direct
integration with our job error

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:47.860
reporting tool, which will
allow you to do more meta

00:28:47.860 --> 00:28:50.710
analysis on specific errors
that might be causing

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:54.260
a backlog in your processing.

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:57.790
So in summary, what we covered
today was ways that you just

00:28:57.790 --> 00:28:59.350
can optimize your
Dataflow pipeline

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:02.410
using the job graph, job
metrics, and the job and worker

00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:03.250
logs.

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:07.390
But not only is it a crucial
tool for optimization but also

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:09.320
for diagnosing problems.

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:12.580
Dataflow pipelines will
always present challenges

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:17.950
due to the shape of data and the
always changing nature of data

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:20.020
processing in real time.

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.390
But the entire
[INAUDIBLE] experience

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:25.600
allows you to get that as
quickly and efficiently as

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:26.100
possible.

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:34.380
So let's summarize what
we've covered today.

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:35.380
How do I start?

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:40.190
You can start with Dataflow
with notebooks or Dataflow SQL.

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:41.357
How do I scale?

00:29:41.357 --> 00:29:43.190
We can scale our knowledge
and organizations

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:44.495
with Flex templates.

00:29:44.495 --> 00:29:45.740
And how do I optimize?

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:47.540
We can maximize the
performance of our jobs

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:51.740
with the new
observability experience.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:53.840
But note that this
is only a fraction

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:55.890
of what Dataflow has to offer.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.360
We can also start our
journey to Dataflow

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:01.810
with Google provided templates,
that I shared in the demo.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:04.970
For scaling, we have Shuffle
and Streaming Engine,

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:06.940
which can scale your
job to millions of QPS

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:09.590
with no code change required.

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:12.770
We have tools to help optimize
your pipelines even more.

00:30:12.770 --> 00:30:16.330
And if you're ready to go take
your pipeline into production,

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:18.220
we published a brand
new white paper

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.560
on building production
ready data pipelines.

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:26.700
To use Dataflow, you have
to use the Apache SDK.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.700
And to that end,
there are lots of ways

00:30:28.700 --> 00:30:31.760
you can build on your
Beam knowledge base.

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:35.090
We have ID exercises
called Katas.

00:30:35.090 --> 00:30:36.900
We have Qwiklabs.

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:38.810
We have code samples.

00:30:38.810 --> 00:30:41.650
And we have a retail
application that's

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:43.520
been open source,
the same application

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:47.070
that I presented
in the third demo.

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:50.500
And not only that, we have a
thriving developer community

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.930
in the Beam world.

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:54.580
This presentation
will be published

00:30:54.580 --> 00:30:56.020
alongside the recording.

00:30:56.020 --> 00:30:57.910
Now, I would strongly
recommend using this

00:30:57.910 --> 00:31:00.560
as a reference for the beginning
of your Beam and Dataflow

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:01.060
journey.

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:05.340
In case you want to
check out what else is

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:08.400
new in data analytics, we
have dozens of sessions

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:09.623
that are ready for you.

00:31:09.623 --> 00:31:12.040
But if you're interested in
more on s streaming analytics,

00:31:12.040 --> 00:31:16.300
I would recommend DA101, Easy
Access to Streaming Analytics

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:20.670
and DA209, Real Time AI,
showing some of our advancements

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:25.600
with Dataflow and machine
learning applications.

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:26.950
Thank you for your time today.

00:31:26.950 --> 00:31:29.400
I'm looking forward to seeing
you start your streaming

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:31.670
journey on Dataflow.

